<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import LifecycleHealthDemo from "$ui/demos/platform/lifecycle/LifecycleHealthDemo.abide"
  import LifecycleErrorDemo from "$ui/demos/platform/lifecycle/LifecycleErrorDemo.abide"
</script>


<main class="page">
  <h1>Lifecycle hooks</h1>
  <p class="lead"><code>src/app.ts</code> exports four lifecycle hooks alongside the middleware onion. <code>onStart(start)</code> and <code>onStop(stop)</code> <em>wrap</em> the real boot/teardown; <code>onHealth()</code> and <code>onError()</code> run per request, inside request scope. The server tab of each card shows this app's real source.</p>
  <Demo id="lifecycle-health" title="onStart + onHealth — boot wrapper &amp; health merge" server="src/app.ts" client="src/ui/demos/platform/lifecycle/LifecycleHealthDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <p><code>onStart(start)</code> runs setup and then <code>await start()</code> to bind the server — the socket binds only <em>inside</em> <code>start()</code>, so nothing serves until setup finishes (returning without calling it aborts boot). Here it seeds a <code>bootId</code> before booting. <code>onHealth()</code> then runs on every <code>GET /__abide/health</code> and merges its fields over the framework stub of <code>reachable</code>, <code>version</code>, <code>startedAt</code>, and <code>uptime</code> — the <code>app</code> + <code>bootId</code> fields below prove both hooks ran. Return <code>reachable: false</code> (or throw) and the endpoint answers 503. <code>onStop(stop)</code> (also in the <code>app.ts</code> source) mirrors <code>onStart</code> for graceful shutdown — no live UI effect.</p>
    <LifecycleHealthDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>health()</code> is isomorphic, so this fetches the live <code>/__abide/health</code> and shows the merged document: the framework stub (<code>reachable</code>, <code>version</code>, <code>startedAt</code>, <code>uptime</code>) plus what <code>onHealth()</code> merged over it. A non-empty <code>bootId</code> is the proof that <code>onStart</code> ran and seeded it before the server bound.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="lifecycle-error" title="onError — shape an uncaught throw" server="src/server/rpc/lifecycleThrow.ts" client="src/ui/demos/platform/lifecycle/LifecycleErrorDemo.abide" tab="server">
    <p>The RPC below <code>throw</code>s an unexpected error — not a typed <code>error()</code> Response. <code>onError(error)</code> (request-scoped) catches it and returns the Response the client sees, instead of an opaque crash. The button posts to it and shows the shaped status + message.</p>
    <LifecycleErrorDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>An RPC that throws. <code>.raw()</code> bypasses the memo and returns the untouched <code>Response</code>, so what you see is exactly the status and body <code>onError</code> shaped — a typed <code>error()</code> would never reach the hook, because it is a Response, not a throw.</p>
  </aside>
</main>
